+## Comparing channels
+
+Delivery apps add delivery and service fees and often price the menu higher than the restaurant does, and they show those fees only at checkout, so the restaurant's own online ordering page is often the cheaper way to order the same food. When you compare channels (see Before you start):
+
+- In step 1, have the helper read every menu source instead of stopping at the first, and report the restaurant's page on each delivery app it is listed on and whether the user is signed in there. In the browser, an app counts as signed in only when its page shows the user's account;
+ a sign-in link or a signed-out notice means it is not. An app with a connected connector counts as signed in through that connector, whatever the browser shows. Decide eligibility from that report and the `SearchPlugins` results, before any cart.
+- Quote every eligible channel, even when one looks cheaper from its menu prices: a membership or promo on a delivery app can erase its fees. Quote up to three channels for the same cart: the restaurant's own online ordering page when it has one, and at most two delivery apps the user is already signed into, in the browser or through a connected connector. Do not compare an app that would need a new account or sign-in;
+ with no eligible channel at all, build no carts and follow Before you start, which says which app to sign into. Only one browser helper runs at a time, so dispatch one helper for all the browser channels: it builds the same cart through step 5 on each channel in turn, in the same tab, and reports for every channel the checkout lines step 5 lists and the checkout page's URL. Each channel keeps its cart when the helper moves on. Tell it to stop at a sign-in, one-time code, or verify-you-are-human wall on a channel, note it, and move on to the next rather than hand it to the user;
+ step 2 runs only for the channel the user picks. A connected connector's quote or preview tool gives its channel's checkout total, and you call it while the helper works. The same connector's order tool places that order in step 9.
+- Compare the checkout totals as each page would charge them, with fees, tax, and tip included, then the ETA or pickup time. A menu price is not a quote, so never ask the user to pick a channel, or pickup versus delivery when they said either is fine, from menu prices: go from the menu straight to the carts, and let the step 6 widget be the only question. When the preselected tips differ, name each channel's tip beside its total. Leave out a channel that stopped at a wall or shows no total before payment, and name it with the reason.
+- In step 6, name the cheapest channel in the widget's prompt with its total, and give each other channel's total and ETA in the summary ("$31.20 on Roberta's own site, ready at 12:40;
+ $38.90 on DoorDash, ETA 12:35"). Add an option for another channel only when it is meaningfully faster.
+- The yes places the order on the channel it chose, by resuming the helper that built the carts for step 9 with that channel's checkout URL: it opens that URL, confirms the items and total match what the widget read back, and only then places it, stopping to report if they differ. Or, for a connector's quote, through that connector's order tool. A connector order skips step 8: it pays only with a way to pay that the connector's quote lists, never a one-time card or the Secure Form, since it has no web checkout to type a card into. Leave the other carts unplaced.
 
